2> <p data-svelte-h=3D"svelte-vbayee">A very popular technique for aligning=
 language models is <strong>Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLH=
F)</strong>. In RLHF, we use human feedback as a proxy for the =E2=80=9Crew=
ard=E2=80=9D signal in RL. Here=E2=80=99s how it works:</p> <ol data-svelte=
-h=3D"svelte-13rt60o"><li><p><strong>Get Human Preferences:</strong> We mig=
ht ask humans to compare different responses generated by the LLM for the s=
ame input prompt and tell us which response they prefer. For example, we mi=
ght show a human two different answers to the question =E2=80=9CWhat is the=
 capital of France?=E2=80=9D and ask them =E2=80=9CWhich answer is better?=
=E2=80=9C.</p></li> <li><p><strong>Train a Reward Model:</strong> We use th=
is human preference data to train a separate model called a <strong>reward =
model</strong>. This reward model learns to predict what kind of responses =
humans will prefer. It learns to score responses based on helpfulness, harm=
lessness, and alignment with human preferences.</p></li> <li><p><strong>Fin=
e-tune the LLM with RL:</strong> Now we use the reward model as the environ=
ment for our LLM agent. The LLM generates responses (actions), and the rewa=
rd model scores these responses (provides rewards). In essence, we=E2=80=99=
re training the LLM to produce text that our reward model (which learned fr=
om human preferences) thinks is good.</p></li></ol> <p data-svelte-h=3D"sve=
lte-20078t"><img src=3D"https://huggingface.co/reasoning-course/images/reso=
lve/main/grpo/2.jpg" alt=3D"RL Basic Concept" data--h-bstatus=3D"5PROCESSED=
" class=3D"undefined gb-blur-image"></p> <p data-svelte-h=3D"svelte-1ljlbgz=
">From a general perspective, let=E2=80=99s look at the benefits of using R=
L in LLMs:</p> <table data-svelte-h=3D"svelte-omrei2"><thead><tr><th>Benefi=
t</th> <th>Description</th></tr></thead> <tbody><tr><td>Improved Control</t=
d> <td>RL allows us to have more control over the kind of text LLMs generat=
e. We can guide them to produce text that is more aligned with specific goa=
ls, like being helpful, creative, or concise.</td></tr> <tr><td>Enhanced Al=
ignment with Human Values</td> <td>RLHF, in particular, helps us align LLMs=
 with complex and often subjective human preferences. It=E2=80=99s hard to =
write down rules for =E2=80=9Cwhat makes a good answer,=E2=80=9D but humans=
 can easily judge and compare responses. RLHF lets the model learn from the=
se human judgments.</td></tr> <tr><td>Mitigating Undesirable Behaviors</td>=
 <td>RL can be used to reduce negative behaviors in LLMs, such as generatin=
g toxic language, spreading misinformation, or exhibiting biases. By design=
ing rewards that penalize these behaviors, we can nudge the model to avoid =
them.</td></tr></tbody></table> <p data-svelte-h=3D"svelte-cocpof">Reinforc=
ement Learning from Human Feedback has been used to train many of the most =
popular LLMs today, such as OpenAI=E2=80=99s GPT-4, Google=E2=80=99s Gemini=
, and DeepSeek=E2=80=99s R1. There are a wide range of techniques for RLHF,=
 with varying degrees of complexity and sophistication. In this chapter, we=
 will focus on Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO), which is a techni=
que for RLHF that has been shown to be effective at training LLMs that are =
helpful, harmless, and aligned with human preferences.</p> <h2 class=3D"rel=
ative group"><a id=3D"why-should-we-care-about-grpo-group-relative-policy-o=
ptimization" class=3D"header-link block pr-1.5 text-lg no-hover:hidden with=
